      content: `The Prophecy: The Old Testament provided multiple prophecies about a forerunner who would prepare the way for the Messiah's coming. Around 740 BC, Isaiah prophesied in [Isaiah 40:3](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Isaiah+40%3A3&version=NIV): "A voice of one calling: 'In the wilderness prepare the way for the Lord; make straight in the desert a highway for our God.'" Three centuries later, around 430 BC, Malachi provided additional details in [Malachi 3:1](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Malachi+3%3A1&version=NIV): "I will send my messenger, who will prepare the way before me. Then suddenly the Lord you are seeking will come to his temple..."[^36] Malachi also promised that God would send "the prophet Elijah" before the day of the Lord ([Malachi 4:5-6](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Malachi+4%3A5-6&version=NIV)).

The Fulfillment: These prophecies found their fulfillment in John the Baptist, whose ministry matched what was prophesied. [Luke 1:13-17](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+1%3A13-17&version=NIV) records the angel Gabriel's announcement to Zacharias that his son would be the prophesied forerunner: "He will go on before the Lord, in the spirit and power of Elijah... to make ready a people prepared for the Lord." When John began his ministry, [Matthew 3:1-3](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+3%3A1-3&version=NIV) explicitly identifies him as the fulfillment of Isaiah's prophecy: "This is he who was spoken of through the prophet Isaiah: 'A voice of one calling in the wilderness...'"[^37]. John's ascetic lifestyle in the wilderness resembled Elijah's[^38]. Jesus confirmed that John was indeed the Elijah who was to come ([Matthew 11:13-14](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+11%3A13-14&version=NIV)).`
